# Day 53

**45km (on canoe) + 5km (walking) / 9 hours**

*John Coull campsite to Ngaporo campsite*

This day was a lot better than yesterday, which came down to a few factors. One was that we got started a lot earlier with much less waiting around. Another was simply that I got a good night's sleep in my tent at the campsite. Also the weather was fantastic.

At the start there was still plenty of logistics with packing the gear into our barrels again but it was more manageable with less food to fit in. Also carrying the barrels and tying everything up just takes a while, much more onerous than packing a backpack.

![The awesome gorge the river is in](./river.avif)

After some time there was a landing at Mangapurua with a short walk to the Bridge to Nowhere. It wasn't an amazing attraction but the walk itself was nice enough, there were plenty of others there with other canoeists and people who came up in jet boats going to see the bridge. Really the point is the river with the spectacular views along the way while the bridge is just a convenient point to have a break and a walk. 

![The bridge to nowhere with some people investigating where nowhere really is](bridge-to-nowhere.avif)

![The view from the bridge](bridge-view.avif)

The afternoon was very hot in the blazing sun, I had got somewhat sunburnt on the previous day and there's nowhere to hide when on the canoe. Covering up is possible but when it's so hot it's hard to do so I just have to use sunblock liberally. Some others even took a short swim in the river to cool off but the river didn't look clean enough to be appealing to me.

From there it was more of the same with some fairly comfortable rapids along to the campsite which was situated in a spectacular area although there were many people there with a large guided tour group as well as our group taking up lots of space with tents but we made it work.

![The view of the river from the campsite with the sun shining on us](./camp-down-river.avif)